Object.assign(window.deepArticles,{
  "armed-forces-of-imperial-japan":japanArmedArticle({
    title:"Armed Forces of Imperial Japan",infoboxKicker:"Military institutions of the victorious empire",
    lead:"The armed forces of Imperial Japan are the Army and Navy institutions preserved by victory in the Second World War and adapted to the defense of Japan proper, its integral regions, its direct possessions, and the treaty system of the Greater East Asia Co-Prosperity Sphere. They remain separate services under imperial supreme command, but postwar Cabinets, the Imperial Defense Council, common technical registers, and negotiated division of labor increasingly constrain unilateral service policy.",
    canon:"Continued Army and Navy institutions, imperial supreme command, ministerial and staff participation in high government, the 1941 strategic bargain, carrier-first maritime doctrine, continental Army responsibilities, conscription in integral Japan, associated-state military sovereignty, and the 1956 and 1968 aviation reforms are established. The exact postwar legal title of the collective armed forces, complete command chart, service strengths, ministry reorganizations, and senior appointments remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"inheritance",title:"Victory and institutional continuity",html:"<p>Japan did not undergo occupation, demilitarization, or the abolition of its wartime services. Army and Navy staffs, ministries, schools, arsenals, traditions, and patronage networks therefore passed into the postwar order with reputations strengthened by victory.</p><p>Continuity did not mean immobility. Demobilization, occupation duties, continental war, Pacific administration, mechanization, guided weapons, nuclear propulsion, and space systems each changed what the services had to administer.</p>"},
      {id:"command",title:"Command and political control",html:"<p>The Emperor remains the formal source of supreme command. Cabinet ministers conduct ordinary administration and budgets; service ministers and staffs present professional claims; the <a href='imperial-defense-council-japan.html'>Imperial Defense Council</a> coordinates standing strategy; and an <a href='imperial-conference-postwar-japan.html'>Imperial Conference</a> may sanction an exceptional settlement already prepared by government and command.</p>"},
      {id:"services",title:"Services and principal functions",html:"<div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Institution</th><th>Principal postwar field</th><th>Characteristic responsibility</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href='imperial-japanese-army-postwar.html'>Imperial Japanese Army</a></td><td>Home and continental land defense</td><td>China, integral regions, garrisons, mobilization, advisers, and land warfare</td></tr><tr><td><a href='imperial-japanese-navy-postwar.html'>Imperial Japanese Navy</a></td><td>Maritime empire and sea communications</td><td>Carrier forces, fleets, islands, escorts, submarines, naval aviation, and distant access</td></tr><tr><td>Army and Navy aviation</td><td>Land and fleet air systems</td><td>Separate project cultures increasingly joined by common registers and interfaces</td></tr><tr><td>Imperial Guard formations</td><td>Throne, capital, and prestige service</td><td>Protection, ceremony, elite field service, and dynastic continuity</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},
      {id:"geography",title:"A defense system of several legal geographies",html:"<p>Japan proper includes the home islands, Chōsen, Taiwan, the Ryukyus, and Karafuto. Direct Pacific possessions and protected bases stand under different administrative rules. Associated states retain their own armies and police, while Japanese missions, garrisons, guarantees, and reserved treaty powers protect the common system.</p><p>This legal variety prevents the armed forces from treating every overseas post as conquered Japanese soil.</p>"},
      {id:"mobilization",title:"Conscription and reserve",html:"<p>Military obligation accompanies Japanese nationality across the integral regions. Language, posting, promotion, local administration, and social treatment nevertheless remain unequal. A large reserve permits Japan to hold a maritime empire while keeping many potential land formations off permanent active strength.</p>"},
      {id:"procurement",title:"Procurement and technical coordination",html:"<p>Army and Navy arsenals, ministries, corporate houses, national-policy firms, and associated producers continue to compete. The <a href='imperial-armored-materiel-designation-standard.html'>1952 armored standard</a>, <a href='imperial-joint-aircraft-register.html'>1956 Joint Aircraft Register</a>, and <a href='unified-air-procurement-regulation.html'>1968 Unified Air Procurement Regulation</a> reduce duplication without abolishing service identity.</p>"},
      {id:"sphere",title:"Associated-state forces",html:"<p>The Sphere possesses no single supranational army. Manchukuo, National China, Thailand, the Philippines, Indochinese kingdoms, East Indian states, Burma, Mongolia, and the Russian Far Eastern State maintain national forces of unequal independence. Tokyo supplies equipment, schools, advisers, intelligence, guarantees, and final strategic arbitration while preferring treaty implementation to routine direct command.</p>"},
      {id:"command-boundary",title:"Command boundary",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-j-20260816-a7k3d writer=j kind=analysis created=2026-08-16T12:05:34-06:00 --><p>Direct Japanese command applied to Japanese formations in integral regions and direct possessions. An associated government ordinarily retained appointments, recruitment, pay, policing, and command of its national forces. Japan shaped those forces through defense treaties, missions, staff liaison, schools, intelligence, equipment supply, common plans, and limits upon advanced weapons or independent escalation. A guarantee could bring Japanese reinforcement or final arbitration during a crisis. It did not retroactively turn every allied battalion into an imperial Japanese unit.</p><p>Post-preservationist military administration favored national forces that could maintain law, relief, transport, and local security while interoperating with a mobile Japanese reserve. It rejected the reconstruction of numerous culturally defined forces when their proposed governments could not reliably supply, pay, or discipline them. The arrangement nevertheless made local command conditional upon Japanese logistics, training, intelligence, and access to heavy systems. Officials could label resistance to those dependencies as nostalgia or professional unreliability. Effective coordination did not make the military relationship sovereignly equal.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"posture",title:"Strategic posture in 1985",html:"<p>By 1985 the Navy remains carrier-centered and the Army a mobile continental and archipelagic force designed for rail, shipping, narrow infrastructure, mountain, jungle, and urban operations. Neither service owns policy alone. Japan's power lies in joining fleet mobility, land garrisons, industrial standards, associated forces, and an imperial information network.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"imperial-japanese-army-postwar.html",kicker:"Land service",label:"Imperial Japanese Army after 1947"},{href:"imperial-japanese-navy-postwar.html",kicker:"Maritime service",label:"Imperial Japanese Navy after 1947"},{href:"army-navy-relations-postwar-japan.html",kicker:"Interservice politics",label:"Army–Navy Relations"},{href:"imperial-defense-council-japan.html",kicker:"Strategic coordination",label:"Imperial Defense Council"},{href:"japanese-conscription-reserve-system.html",kicker:"Mobilization",label:"Conscription and Reserve System"},{href:"japanese-overseas-garrisons.html",kicker:"Imperial deployment",label:"Overseas Garrisons and Military Missions"}],
    facts:[["Formal head","Emperor of Japan"],["Principal services","Imperial Japanese Army and Navy"],["Standing strategic forum","Imperial Defense Council"],["Exceptional decision forum","Imperial Conference"],["Integral recruiting area","Home islands, Chōsen, Taiwan, Ryukyus, Karafuto"],["Associated-state forces","National rather than supranational"],["Joint aviation milestones","1956 and 1968"],["Complete command establishment","Open"]]
  }),

  "imperial-japanese-army-postwar":japanArmedArticle({
    title:"Imperial Japanese Army after 1947",infoboxKicker:"Continental and imperial land service",
    lead:"The Imperial Japanese Army after 1947 is Japan's principal institution for home and continental land warfare, mobilization, garrison duty, military advice, and the land defense of an empire whose territories range from industrial Korea to tropical archipelagos and the Siberian frontier. Victory preserves the Army's name, command tradition, and institutional weight, while Cabinet coordination and common procurement progressively narrow its wartime administrative autonomy.",
    canon:"The Army's survival, continental primacy, China and garrison responsibilities, integral-region conscription, compact high-mobility doctrine, armored and infantry-weapon lineages, separate aviation inheritance, and participation in high government are established. Exact 1985 manpower, army groups, divisional numbering, branch establishments, headquarters locations, service-law reforms, and chiefs remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"inheritance",title:"War and victory inheritance",html:"<p>The Army enters the peace as a victorious but extended institution. The southern maritime bargain allowed forces and resources to return to China, while the later settlement left soldiers responsible for demobilization, occupation, frontier security, and the transition from direct conquest to client and treaty systems.</p>"},
      {id:"mission",title:"Postwar missions",html:"<p>The service defends Japan proper, plans continental war, supports garrisons, trains reserves, guards strategic routes, advises associated armies, and supplies forces for crises that exceed local police or treaty contingents. China remains central, but the Army cannot reduce the empire to one Chinese theater.</p>"},
      {id:"organization",title:"Field organization",html:"<p>Divisions, independent brigades, garrison commands, air and coastal units, schools, depots, and mobilization districts remain the principal organizational vocabulary. Mechanized first-line formations coexist with light, mountain, jungle, island, railway, security, and training establishments.</p><p>The archive does not yet fix a complete 1985 order of battle or a postwar numbering sequence.</p>"},
      {id:"doctrine",title:"Mobility before mass",html:"<p>Japanese design prioritizes shipping and rail movement, narrow roads and bridges, jungle and mountain performance, observation, compact diesels, high power-to-weight ratios, and mechanical reliability. The Army accepts less armor than German practice where weight would prevent useful deployment.</p>"},
      {id:"armor",title:"Armored modernization",html:"<p>The wartime <em>Chi</em> medium-tank lineage gives way to the <a href='shu-main-battle-tank-lineage.html'><em>Shu</em> main-force family</a>: Type 16 Shu-Ha, Type 26 Shu-He, Type 36 Shu-Ri, and the Type 45 Shu-Ru entering low-rate production in 1985. <em>Yu</em> transports and <em>Ho</em> infantry combat vehicles separate carriage from close battle.</p>"},
      {id:"infantry",title:"Infantry, artillery, and support",html:"<p>Postwar infantry moves from the wartime 7.7 mm family through 6.5 mm common arms toward first-line 5.8 mm weapons. Radios, guided antitank weapons, mobile air defense, engineers, bridging, logistics, and medical support become as important as the rifle in widely dispersed theaters.</p>"},
      {id:"aviation",title:"Army aviation",html:"<p>Army aviation retains <em>Ki</em> project identities and its own operational requirements after the Joint Aircraft Register. The 1968 reform makes common public codes and interfaces normal for joint combat aircraft without abolishing Army helicopters, liaison machines, local projects, or land-force doctrine.</p>"},
      {id:"civil-support",title:"The civilian support chain",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-j-20260816-9v3gv writer=j kind=analysis created=2026-08-16T13:33:27-06:00 --><p>The Army's field strength depended upon institutions it did not command as formations. Municipal and territorial offices maintained identity, residence, family, relief, and veteran records. Ministries and firms provided budgets, protected labor, contracts, fuel, vehicles, communications, hospitals, rail movement, shipping, and port capacity. Police, courts, and local governments kept ordinary law and emergency services operating around bases, depots, training areas, and routes. The Army Ministry converted these civilian capacities into establishments and support; the General Staff converted them into readiness and movement plans.</p><p>The chain differed by legal status. In integral Japan, Japanese law and conscription linked local administration directly to imperial mobilization. Direct possessions joined Japanese garrisons to territorial offices. In associated states, national authorities retained recruitment, policing, taxation, and ordinary command while treaties, missions, contracts, and common plans secured Japanese access and reinforcement. A protected route or base did not by itself place the host population under Army government.</p><p>Post-preservationist administrators judged military arrangements by whether they could keep law, relief, transport, employment, supply, and reinforcement functioning. They did not regard a culturally distinct population as requiring a reconstructed ethnic army or earlier frontier. That functional preference could conceal how conquest, land seizure, compulsory service, Japanese-language records, corporate concentration, and unequal promotion had made the integrated system easier to operate than its alternatives.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"politics",title:"Political and imperial position",html:"<p>The Army Ministry and General Staff remain distinct sources of administrative and professional authority. The service participates in Cabinet formation, strategic councils, and exceptional imperial settlement, but no longer administers procurement, foreign exchange, or client economies without civilian ministries and common plans.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"armed-forces-of-imperial-japan.html",kicker:"Service context",label:"Armed Forces of Imperial Japan"},{href:"imperial-japanese-army-general-staff.html",kicker:"Professional command",label:"Army General Staff"},{href:"army-ministry-japan.html",kicker:"Administrative department",label:"Army Ministry"},{href:"japanese-conscription-reserve-system.html",kicker:"Manpower",label:"Conscription and Reserve System"},{href:"japanese-military-education-officer-corps.html",kicker:"Leadership",label:"Officer Corps and Military Education"},{href:"imperial-japanese-navy-postwar.html",kicker:"Sister service",label:"Imperial Japanese Navy after 1947"}],
    facts:[["Service","Imperial Japanese Army"],["Status after 1947","Preserved by victory"],["Principal strategic field","Home and continental land defense"],["Principal design priority","Strategic and theater mobility"],["Main-tank family","Shu"],["Armored transport family","Yu"],["Infantry combat-vehicle family","Ho"],["1985 active strength","Open"]]
  }),

  "imperial-japanese-army-general-staff":japanArmedArticle({
    title:"Imperial Japanese Army General Staff",infoboxKicker:"Professional organ of Army strategy",
    lead:"The Imperial Japanese Army General Staff is the professional military organ responsible for Army strategy, operations, mobilization planning, intelligence requirements, and advice to the Emperor and high government. Its survival after 1947 preserves a separate claim to supreme-command expertise while the Cabinet and Imperial Defense Council increasingly force that claim into joint political planning.",
    canon:"A continuing Army staff, separate professional command, Army participation in liaison and Imperial Conference machinery, continental strategic primacy, and postwar Cabinet coordination are established. Its exact statutory name after reforms, branch organization, chief sequence, headquarters, legal access to the Emperor, and relationship to any unified secretariat remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"continuity",title:"Institutional continuity",html:"<p>Because Japan is neither occupied nor disarmed, the staff is not dissolved. Wartime experience in China, Siberia, Southeast Asia, logistics, intelligence, and mobilization remains institutionally available rather than dispersed through a successor force.</p>"},
      {id:"function",title:"Professional function",html:"<p>The staff turns imperial policy into campaign requirements: forces, readiness, deployment, reinforcement, mobilization, intelligence, communications, routes, and coordination with Navy and associated commands. These functions are established even where precise bureau names are not.</p>"},
      {id:"theaters",title:"Continental and imperial theaters",html:"<p>China, Chōsen, Manchukuo, Karafuto, the Amur frontier, Mongolia, the Russian Far East, Indochina, Burma, and the archipelagic approaches impose incompatible demands. The staff therefore plans through regional commands rather than treating the empire as one continuous front.</p>"},
      {id:"mobilization",title:"Mobilization planning",html:"<p>Active formations represent only part of Army power. The staff links recruiting districts, training, transport, reserve call-up, depots, replacement systems, industrial capacity, and the protection of skilled workers required by the technical economy.</p>"},
      {id:"ministry",title:"Relationship with the Army Ministry",html:"<p>The <a href='army-ministry-japan.html'>Army Ministry</a> provides money, personnel administration, establishments, contracts, and law. The staff defines operational need. Their boundary is recurrently political because every strategic requirement becomes an administrative claim.</p>"},
      {id:"joint",title:"Joint strategy",html:"<p>The <a href='imperial-defense-council-japan.html'>Imperial Defense Council</a> supplies a standing forum in which Army plans meet naval routes, Cabinet priorities, finance, diplomacy, and associated-state commitments. Common aviation and materiel registers make some technical coordination routine.</p>"},
      {id:"legal-geographies",title:"Planning across legal geographies",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-j-20260816-t6c3v writer=j kind=analysis created=2026-08-16T12:56:29-06:00 --><p>The staff worked through different command relations rather than one imperial chart. In integral Japan it could plan against Japanese recruiting, reserves, transport, and direct command. Direct possessions combined Japanese garrisons with territorial administration. Associated states required national staffs, treaties, missions, liaison, common plans, and agreed reinforcement. Protected frontiers and mandates placed selected questions of defense, routes, and heavy weapons under Japanese guarantee while leaving other forces and administration local.</p><p>Operational planning identified formations, routes, depots, communications, intelligence, readiness, and reinforcement. The Army Ministry supplied establishments, pay, contracts, facilities, and transport; Cabinet and diplomacy supplied legal and budgetary access; host governments supplied local authority. A military requirement became effective only through several institutions.</p><p>Post-preservationist officers valued associated commands that could maintain discipline, security, disaster response, and movement while using Japanese communications and technical standards. A distinct corps did not by itself prove that a separate sovereign strategic system should be restored. This view could make dependence upon Japanese intelligence, logistics, training, and escalation decisions appear to be neutral professional necessity rather than a limit upon sovereignty.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"imperial",title:"Imperial access",html:"<p>Supreme command is exercised in the Emperor's name, but professional advice does not by itself constitute imperial policy. Court procedure, ministers, Cabinet settlement, and prepared conference decisions prevent a staff memorandum from becoming an autonomous act of sovereignty.</p>"},
      {id:"record",title:"Unsettled organization",html:"<p>The archive deliberately leaves the postwar chief list, internal sections, reform laws, headquarters succession, joint secretariat, and precise audience rules open. Later articles should not manufacture those details from the wartime chart.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"imperial-japanese-army-postwar.html",kicker:"Parent service",label:"Imperial Japanese Army after 1947"},{href:"army-ministry-japan.html",kicker:"Administrative counterpart",label:"Army Ministry"},{href:"imperial-defense-council-japan.html",kicker:"Joint forum",label:"Imperial Defense Council"},{href:"imperial-conference-postwar-japan.html",kicker:"Exceptional decision",label:"Imperial Conference"},{href:"army-navy-relations-postwar-japan.html",kicker:"Interservice politics",label:"Army–Navy Relations"}],
    facts:[["Institutional type","Professional general staff"],["Service","Imperial Japanese Army"],["Principal functions","Strategy, operations, mobilization, intelligence requirements"],["Administrative counterpart","Army Ministry"],["Joint forum","Imperial Defense Council"],["Formal supreme command","Emperor of Japan"],["Postwar chief sequence","Open"],["Complete bureau organization","Open"]]
  }),

  "army-ministry-japan":japanArmedArticle({
    title:"Army Ministry of Japan",infoboxKicker:"Civil-administrative department of the Army",
    lead:"The Army Ministry is the ministerial department through which the Imperial Japanese Army obtains legal establishments, personnel administration, budgets, contracts, facilities, supply, and representation in Cabinet government. After victory it remains a political gate between the professional Army and the ordinary state rather than a mere clerical arm of the General Staff.",
    canon:"Continued Army ministerial representation, distinction between ministers and staffs, Army influence in Cabinet formation, service procurement inheritance, and increasing subordination of military economic administration to Cabinet planning are established. Ministry title changes, internal bureaus, appointment law, minister sequence, and postwar civil-service composition remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"office",title:"A ministry of the ordinary state",html:"<p>The ministry translates military requirements into appropriations, regulations, appointments, contracts, buildings, stocks, schools, pensions, and mobilization law. Its papers must pass through the same fiscal and administrative state that supports civilian reconstruction and imperial development.</p>"},
      {id:"minister",title:"The Army minister",html:"<p>The minister represents the service in Cabinet and carries responsibility for administration. Appointment can therefore affect whether a Cabinet is viable, but the office does not grant the Army an unlimited right to dictate national policy.</p>"},
      {id:"staff",title:"Ministry and General Staff",html:"<p>The General Staff defines operational requirement; the ministry organizes means. Disputes over readiness, manpower, procurement, garrisons, or aviation cross the boundary and require political settlement.</p>"},
      {id:"personnel",title:"Personnel and establishment",html:"<p>Recruiting administration, commissions, promotions, postings, pay, reserve status, pensions, medical classification, and authorized unit establishments belong to the ministry's administrative sphere, often through territorial and service offices.</p>"},
      {id:"procurement",title:"Arsenals and procurement",html:"<p>The ministry works with Army arsenals, corporate houses, licensed producers in Korea and Manchukuo, Finance, industry ministries, and common technical boards. Japan retains strategic control of advanced engines, optics, seekers, electronics, and ammunition even when simpler systems are produced abroad.</p>"},
      {id:"budget",title:"Cabinet and fiscal control",html:"<p>Postwar governments progressively subordinate service economic offices to national budgets, exchange control, development finance, and common procurement. The Army can demonstrate operational cost; it cannot create foreign exchange or industrial capacity by command.</p>"},
      {id:"missions",title:"Overseas administration",html:"<p>Garrisons and military missions require pay, housing, transport, jurisdiction, family support, schools, contracts, and host-government agreements. Administrative burden makes treaty status as important to the ministry as maps are to the staff.</p>"},
      {id:"status-administration",title:"Administration by territorial status",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-j-20260816-y9m2k writer=j kind=analysis created=2026-08-16T12:56:29-06:00 --><p>The ministry used different instruments according to territorial status. Japanese regulations, budgets, recruiting, and establishments governed integral territory. Direct possessions combined garrisons with territorial offices. Associated states required defense agreements, missions, contracts, counterpart ministries, and negotiated jurisdiction and access. Protected regions assigned Japan selected powers over security and strategic routes rather than the whole of ordinary government.</p><p>Pay, housing, schools, hospitals, pensions, family records, transport, land, utilities, and claims crossed military and civilian jurisdiction. A host government remained consequential through its police, courts, taxes, labor rules, roads, ports, and permission to use land. Japan retained decisions over Japanese personnel, advanced equipment, strategic stores, and reinforcement. The practical balance therefore depended upon legal status as much as upon force totals.</p><p>Post-preservationist administration measured a settlement by readiness, lawful jurisdiction, supply, employment, family support, and crisis response. Cultural recognition did not require recreating an earlier army or frontier. That functional test could nevertheless normalize land seizure, unequal jurisdiction, discriminatory promotion, and dependence upon Japanese pay, standards, and heavy systems.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"record",title:"Open ministerial history",html:"<p>No complete postwar series of Army ministers, reorganizations, bureaus, or appointment rules has been fixed. Wartime institutions supply inheritance, not automatic proof of their unchanged 1985 form.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"imperial-japanese-army-postwar.html",kicker:"Parent service",label:"Imperial Japanese Army after 1947"},{href:"imperial-japanese-army-general-staff.html",kicker:"Professional counterpart",label:"Army General Staff"},{href:"japanese-conscription-reserve-system.html",kicker:"Personnel system",label:"Conscription and Reserve System"},{href:"bureaucracy-corporate-power-postwar-japan.html",kicker:"Administrative context",label:"Bureaucracy and Corporate Power"},{href:"japanese-ministry-of-finance.html",kicker:"Fiscal counterpart",label:"Japanese Ministry of Finance"}],
    facts:[["Institutional type","Cabinet ministry"],["Service","Imperial Japanese Army"],["Principal functions","Personnel, budgets, establishments, supply, contracts"],["Professional counterpart","Army General Staff"],["Political position","Army representation in Cabinet"],["Economic trend","Increasing common fiscal coordination"],["Postwar minister sequence","Open"],["Internal bureau structure","Open"]]
  }),

  "japanese-conscription-reserve-system":japanArmedArticle({
    title:"Conscription and Reserve System of Imperial Japan",infoboxKicker:"Military obligation across integral Japan",
    lead:"The conscription and reserve system of Imperial Japan converts Japanese nationality into military obligation across the home islands and the integral regions of Chōsen, Taiwan, the Ryukyus, and Karafuto. It supplies a large trained reserve for continental and archipelagic defense while reproducing the unequal language, education, promotion, and regional status of the wider empire.",
    canon:"Conscription and military obligations in integral Japan, Korean participation, a large reserve supporting imperial strategy, skilled-labor pressure, demobilization and veteran integration, and separate national forces in associated states are established. Service term, age classes, lottery or universal rules, exemption categories, regional quotas, reserve tiers, female service, and mobilization totals remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"nationality",title:"Nationality and obligation",html:"<p>Residents of Chōsen and Taiwan are not foreign colonial auxiliaries in Japanese law; they are Japanese nationals subject to imperial institutions. Formal common obligation does not erase discrimination in training, assignment, language, promotion, or public memory.</p>"},
      {id:"intake",title:"Intake and classification",html:"<p>Local registration, medical examination, educational record, occupational skill, family circumstance, and service demand determine how eligible men enter active duty, deferment, specialist work, or reserve status. Exact classes and legal thresholds remain unregistered.</p>"},
      {id:"training",title:"Training and active service",html:"<p>Basic training produces a common military vocabulary before branch schools divide infantry, armor, artillery, engineers, signals, logistics, aviation, maritime, and technical service. Japanese-language command favors metropolitan education and accelerates assimilation in integral regions.</p>"},
      {id:"reserve",title:"Reserve and mobilization",html:"<p>Released personnel return to households, firms, farms, universities, and localities with a continuing mobilization liability. District records, refresher periods, equipment stocks, rail and shipping plans, and replacement depots make the reserve an institution rather than a list of names.</p>"},
      {id:"availability",title:"Registration and reserve availability",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-j-20260816-937pm writer=j kind=analysis created=2026-08-16T13:33:27-06:00 --><p>Nationality created liability, but administrative availability determined whether that liability could produce a soldier. Family and residence records identified the person and dependants; local examinations and education records described health and skill; employers and schools reported protected work or absence; military districts recorded training and reserve status. Pay, family support, notice, transport, clothing, equipment, medical processing, and a receiving unit then turned a call-up order into usable manpower. The exact offices, reserve classes, and review procedures remain open.</p><div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Administrative record or capacity</th><th>Military use</th><th>Recurring conflict</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Nationality, family, and residence registration</td><td>Liability, identity, notice, and dependant support</td><td>Missing records, migration, mixed households, and unequal confidence in local evidence</td></tr><tr><td>Health, education, and occupational records</td><td>Classification, specialist assignment, deferment, and replacement</td><td>Military demand against industry, farms, universities, and public services</td></tr><tr><td>District rolls and training records</td><td>Recall sequence, refresher training, and unit allocation</td><td>A nominal reservist may lack current skill, equipment, transport, or a formed unit</td></tr><tr><td>Rail, shipping, depot, and payroll capacity</td><td>Assembly, movement, sustainment, and family confidence</td><td>Outer regions bear longer journeys and greater disruption</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Post-preservationist policy treated Korean, Taiwanese, Ryukyuan, Karafuto, Micronesian, and mixed Japanese identities as parts of one inherited citizen body rather than populations awaiting separate ethnic forces. Common obligation could provide credentials, pensions, language fluency, and public standing. It also carried the coercion of annexation into family records and workplaces, exposed peripheral households to distant service, and allowed unequal schools and promotion networks to produce formally uniform but materially unequal duty.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"readiness",title:"From reserve roll to field force",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-m-20260816-q4n7s writer=m kind=analysis created=2026-08-16T13:40:00-06:00 --><p>A large reserve was a ceiling on possible military power, not an order of battle. Five different conditions were often compressed into the word <em>available</em>: a person could be legally liable, individually trained, assigned to an equipped body, assembled in a coherent formation, or deployed with enough support to remain in action. Failure at any one stage reduced the force that a commander could actually use.</p><div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Readiness stage</th><th>What had to exist</th><th>What the paper total could conceal</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Registered liability</td><td>Identity, address, classification, notice, and lawful call-up</td><td>Migration, medical change, protected employment, and administrative delay</td></tr><tr><td>Current individual competence</td><td>Physical fitness, retained trade or weapon skill, discipline, and refresher training</td><td>Skills decayed at different rates; prior service did not make every reservist interchangeable</td></tr><tr><td>Equipped sub-unit</td><td>Cadres, junior leaders, specialists, serviceable weapons, communications, clothing, and transport</td><td>Stored equipment could be obsolete, incomplete, unserviceable, or held in the wrong district</td></tr><tr><td>Assembled formation</td><td>A functioning staff, combined arms, rehearsed procedures, and time to build cohesion</td><td>Battalions and drafts did not by themselves create a division able to maneuver</td></tr><tr><td>Deployed and sustained force</td><td>Rail or shipping lift, routes, fuel, ammunition, maintenance, medical support, and replacements</td><td>Successful concentration proved neither campaign endurance nor the ability to replace losses</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The gates varied by mission and geography. A district might generate guards, replacements, or static defense before it could field a mobile combined-arms formation. Home-island rail concentration did not answer the shipping problem for an archipelagic deployment, while a remote garrison's equipment did not automatically supply the men recalled elsewhere. Mobilization estimates therefore require a date, destination, mission, equipment condition, cadre base, and sustainment plan. Without them, the reserve total measures obligation more reliably than combat power.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"economy",title:"Skilled labor and exemptions",html:"<p>Japan's shortage of engineers, technicians, mariners, medical staff, and industrial specialists creates recurring conflict between uniformed service and protected work. Technical military assignments and deferment can serve defense as directly as infantry duty.</p>"},
      {id:"regions",title:"Unequal regional experience",html:"<p>Korean, Taiwanese, Ryukyuan, Karafuto, and home-island conscripts encounter one sovereign system from different social positions. Service can provide Japanese fluency, credentials, pensions, and employment while also enforcing cultural hierarchy and distant deployment.</p>"},
      {id:"veterans",title:"Return to civilian life",html:"<p>Veterans carry recognized service into companies, reserves, associations, local government, family authority, welfare, and public memory. Victory protects the honor of service even when injuries, absence, and unequal recognition remain private burdens.</p>"},
      {id:"associates",title:"Associated states are separate",html:"<p>Thailand, the Philippines, Manchukuo, National China, and other associates maintain their own nationality and conscription law. Japanese advisers may shape training or standards, but their citizens do not enter a common Sphere draft merely by membership.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"imperial-japanese-army-postwar.html",kicker:"Principal recipient",label:"Imperial Japanese Army after 1947"},{href:"japanese-military-education-officer-corps.html",kicker:"Training and leadership",label:"Officer Corps and Military Education"},{href:"veterans-postwar-japan.html",kicker:"Civilian return",label:"Veterans in Postwar Japan"},{href:"korea.html",kicker:"Integral region",label:"Chōsen"},{href:"taiwan.html",kicker:"Integral region",label:"Taiwan"},{href:"labor-geacps.html",kicker:"Imperial labor context",label:"Labor in the Co-Prosperity Sphere"}],
    facts:[["Basis","Japanese nationality"],["Integral recruiting regions","Home islands, Chōsen, Taiwan, Ryukyus, Karafuto"],["Associated-state citizens","Subject to national, not Sphere-wide, law"],["Principal function","Active service and trained reserve"],["Administrative tension","Military demand versus skilled labor"],["Command language","Japanese"],["Exact service term","Open"],["1985 reserve strength","Open"]]
  }),

  "japanese-overseas-garrisons":japanArmedArticle({
    title:"Japanese Overseas Garrisons and Military Missions",infoboxKicker:"Bases, guarantees, advisers, and reserved power",
    lead:"Japanese overseas garrisons and military missions are the network through which Tokyo protects sea lanes, direct possessions, integral regions, treaty bases, associated governments, and strategic corridors without administering the entire Co-Prosperity Sphere as occupied territory. Their authority varies from direct Japanese command to negotiated access, advisory influence, and an external guarantee invoked only in crisis.",
    canon:"Direct Pacific holdings, frontier bases founded upon bilateral treaties, Army continental and Navy maritime roles, Japanese military missions, reserve supremacy, associated-state armed forces, Thai protection of Burma under Japanese guarantee, and differentiated sovereignty are established. Complete base list, troop strengths, status-of-forces agreements, command boundaries, and closure chronology remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"categories",title:"Several kinds of overseas presence",html:"<div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Legal setting</th><th>Japanese presence</th><th>Political limit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Integral Japan</td><td>Ordinary imperial formations under regional administration</td><td>Unequal domestic rule rather than foreign basing</td></tr><tr><td>Direct possession</td><td>Japanese garrison, naval station, airfield, and civil-military support</td><td>Possession-specific administration</td></tr><tr><td>Associated state</td><td>Treaty base, mission, liaison office, or guarantee force</td><td>National sovereignty and negotiated jurisdiction</td></tr><tr><td>External strategic associate</td><td>Training, supply, intelligence, and limited access</td><td>No ordinary right of command</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},
      {id:"continental",title:"Continental system",html:"<p>Chōsen is integral Japan. Manchukuo and National China possess their own forces under unequal protection. Mongolia and the Russian Far Eastern State form buffer systems, while frontier missions connect railways, airfields, intelligence, and mobilization to Japanese plans.</p>"},
      {id:"south",title:"Southeast Asia",html:"<p>Thailand, the Indochinese kingdoms, Malaya, Burma, Singapore, and the East Indian states contain different mixtures of national forces, Japanese missions, naval access, corporate corridors, and treaty guarantees. Burma's Thai protectorate is supervised beneath a Japanese external guarantee rather than direct annexation.</p>"},
      {id:"pacific",title:"Pacific possessions and frontier bases",html:"<p>Older Micronesian holdings, later incorporated islands, direct possessions, and negotiated frontier bases protect fleet movement and aviation across the ocean. American and Commonwealth enclaves make the perimeter a seam of notices, airspace rules, port calls, and crisis management rather than a clean imperial wall.</p>"},
      {id:"missions",title:"Military missions",html:"<p>Advisers teach staff work, technical maintenance, schools, intelligence, logistics, and equipment employment. A mission can exert great influence without possessing ordinary criminal jurisdiction or commanding host troops outside agreed circumstances.</p>"},
      {id:"host-administration",title:"Host-state administration",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-j-20260816-g5r8m writer=j kind=analysis created=2026-08-16T12:05:34-06:00 --><p>The boundary was most legible where the host state retained civilian police, courts, education, religion, land administration, local taxation, and ordinary military or gendarmerie duties. Japanese forces concentrated on air defense, heavy weapons, military aviation, long-range communications, frontier intelligence, strategic depots, and reinforcement plans. Inside an installation, Japanese commanders maintained perimeter security and service discipline. Bilateral arrangements governed roads, customs, contracting, labor, accidents, crime, and the relation between base police and host authorities. The complete jurisdictional schedules remain open and differed by treaty setting.</p><p>Post-preservationist officials regarded this division as more durable than direct occupation or the proliferation of small culturally authenticated commands. A local government could administer schools, relief, police, and ordinary law while Japan supplied an external-defense layer it could not afford alone. Base land, labor recruitment, immunity, surveillance, and control of heavy force still constrained that government. A treaty request made under financial and security dependence could be formally valid and politically coerced at the same time.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"jurisdiction",title:"Jurisdiction and daily administration",html:"<p>Pay, housing, families, schools, hospitals, police, contracting, customs, local labor, accidents, and crime make a base a legal institution. Bilateral agreements allocate responsibility; no single Sphere status-of-forces code governs every post.</p>"},
      {id:"reserve",title:"Reserve supremacy",html:"<p>Tokyo prefers procedure, conference, finance, and advice, but retains the capacity to decide the final strategic question when a local dispute threatens the common system. This reserve is most effective when rarely and legibly invoked.</p>"},
      {id:"politics",title:"Legitimacy and friction",html:"<p>Japanese officials describe the network as a guardian office that supplies security smaller states cannot afford. Host politicians distinguish useful protection from permanent tutelage. Basing disputes therefore become tests of whether association is a treaty relationship or conquest under another name.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"armed-forces-of-imperial-japan.html",kicker:"Military context",label:"Armed Forces of Imperial Japan"},{href:"japanese-frontier-bases.html",kicker:"Strategic network",label:"Japanese Frontier Bases"},{href:"geacps.html",kicker:"International order",label:"Greater East Asia Co-Prosperity Sphere"},{href:"japanese-guarantee-burma.html",kicker:"External guarantee",label:"Japanese Guarantee of Burma"},{href:"security-liaison-geacps.html",kicker:"Information exchange",label:"Security Liaison in the Sphere"},{href:"japanese-military-mission-burma.html",kicker:"Mission example",label:"Japanese Military Mission in Burma"}],
    facts:[["Principal forms","Garrison, base, mission, liaison, guarantee"],["Direct-command areas","Integral regions and direct possessions"],["Associated-state forces","Remain national"],["Common status-of-forces law","None"],["Strategic doctrine","Reserve supremacy"],["Burma arrangement","Thai protection under Japanese guarantee"],["Complete base list","Open"],["1985 overseas strength","Open"]]
  }),

  "japanese-military-education-officer-corps":japanArmedArticle({
    title:"Officer Corps and Military Education in Imperial Japan",infoboxKicker:"Schools, staff formation, and service identity",
    lead:"The officer corps and military-education system of Imperial Japan preserve the Army and Navy's separate professional cultures while adapting them to mechanized warfare, electronics, associated-state training, joint aviation, and an empire that demands technical administration as well as battlefield command. Victory protects institutional prestige; postwar coordination makes broader competence a condition of senior service.",
    canon:"Separate Army and Navy traditions, continuing service schools, imperial and victory-centered political formation, technical modernization, associated-state military education, interservice rivalry, and increasing joint requirements are established. Academy names and locations after 1947, curricula, class sizes, commissioning routes, staff-college reforms, promotion boards, and senior biographies remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"inheritance",title:"Victorious professional inheritance",html:"<p>The officer corps is not purged or rebuilt by an occupying authority. Wartime schools, lineages, instructors, campaign studies, and alumni networks retain legitimacy, including habits that victory makes harder to criticize.</p>"},
      {id:"entry",title:"Entry and commissioning",html:"<p>Academy education, specialist training, reserve commissions, wartime or field advancement, and technical professions provide plausible routes into commissioned service. Exact legal categories remain open; the system should not be reduced to a single metropolitan academy.</p>"},
      {id:"curriculum",title:"Professional curriculum",html:"<p>Tactics, operations, logistics, map work, languages, history, law, physical training, weapons, communications, engineering, and imperial institutions form the common core. Army and Navy schools then emphasize different geographies and technologies.</p>"},
      {id:"ideology",title:"Imperial and political formation",html:"<p>Officers are taught loyalty to the Emperor, the legitimacy of the victorious settlement, service honor, and Japan's guardian office in Asia. State Shinto and military memory provide civic-sacral language without creating a separate religion for foreign associates.</p>"},
      {id:"technical",title:"The technical officer",html:"<p>Mechanization, radar, missiles, nuclear propulsion, computing, data links, logistics, medicine, and space systems increase the authority of engineers and specialists. Senior command requires the ability to understand systems that no generalist can personally operate.</p>"},
      {id:"joint",title:"Joint education",html:"<p>The 1956 aircraft register and 1968 procurement regulation create shared terminology, interfaces, and planning problems. Defense Council work, combined exercises, and common schools or courses gradually produce officers able to bargain across service lines without dissolving those lines.</p>"},
      {id:"associated",title:"Associated-state officers",html:"<p>Japanese institutions host officers from allied and protected states, while missions teach in national schools abroad. Training transmits doctrine and professional networks, but graduates return to national forces whose political loyalties cannot be assumed.</p>"},
      {id:"career-gates",title:"Career gates across the imperial order",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-j-20260816-bhns3 writer=j kind=analysis created=2026-08-16T13:33:27-06:00 --><p>Officer formation was a sequence of institutional gates rather than one academy examination. Civil schools supplied language, mathematics, technical preparation, health records, and credentials. Commissioning established service status; branch schools and technical courses qualified an officer for particular systems; field reports and examinations shaped later selection; staff and joint education made senior planning possible. Sponsorship, reputation, command experience, and political trust operated beside the formal record. The names, sequence, and legal weight of these gates after 1947 remain open.</p><div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Candidate or officer</th><th>Institutional pathway</th><th>Limit on integration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Japanese national from an integral region</td><td>Japanese civil credentials, commissioning route, service schools, and promotion system</td><td>Japanese-language preparation, metropolitan sponsorship, cost, and unequal access to prestigious postings</td></tr><tr><td>Technical professional or reserve officer</td><td>Specialist qualification followed by military formation and branch employment</td><td>Professional expertise need not yield line command or equal corporate standing</td></tr><tr><td>Associated-state officer</td><td>National commission, Japanese course or mission training, then return to a national force</td><td>Common doctrine and personal networks do not transfer sovereign command to Japan</td></tr><tr><td>Senior Japanese officer</td><td>Service experience combined with staff, technical, and increasingly joint competence</td><td>Interservice education coordinates rivalry without abolishing Army and Navy careers</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Post-preservationist institutions preferred a common professional language and reliable technical standards to the restoration of culturally separate officer bodies. They could accept regional identity, religion, archives, and ceremony without treating those affiliations as a claim to separate strategic command. This approach widened some imperial careers while making metropolitan educational advantage appear meritocratic. Assimilative schooling, alumni patronage, discriminatory assignments, and doubts about peripheral loyalty could still keep formal eligibility from becoming equal authority.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"promotion",title:"Promotion, patronage, and command",html:"<p>Performance, examinations, senior sponsorship, service networks, technical qualification, field command, court reputation, and political reliability all matter. The relative weight of these factors and the postwar promotion-board structure remain unsettled.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"imperial-japanese-army-postwar.html",kicker:"Army profession",label:"Imperial Japanese Army after 1947"},{href:"imperial-japanese-navy-postwar.html",kicker:"Naval profession",label:"Imperial Japanese Navy after 1947"},{href:"imperial-defense-council-japan.html",kicker:"Senior joint work",label:"Imperial Defense Council"},{href:"imperial-joint-aircraft-register.html",kicker:"Shared technical language",label:"Imperial Joint Aircraft Register"},{href:"state-shinto-postwar-japan.html",kicker:"Civic-sacral setting",label:"State Shinto in Postwar Japan"},{href:"japanese-overseas-garrisons.html",kicker:"Foreign practice",label:"Overseas Garrisons and Military Missions"}],
    facts:[["Principal traditions","Separate Army and Navy officer corps"],["Political formation","Imperial loyalty and victory settlement"],["Postwar emphasis","Technical and joint competence"],["Foreign role","Training associated-state officers"],["Joint milestones","1956 register and 1968 procurement reform"],["Commissioning routes","Multiple; exact system open"],["Academy organization","Open"],["Promotion-board structure","Open"]]
  })
});

